        "title": "Are you listening effectively?",
        "text": "A  Listening is a badly neglected communication skill. Many students think that because they can hear, they are listening. But letting words pour into your ear is not listening. And yet listening is the most used method of learning.\n\nB  Lead rather than follow. Leading involves two steps. First, read the assignments you are given before you come to class: if you read before you hear the lecture, you will be more alert to the important ideas. Second, set up questions to keep yourself in the lead. These are not questions that you ask your instructor, but ones around which you plan your listening.\n\nC  Look for the important ideas. Most lecturers will introduce a few new ideas and give explanations, examples or other support for them. Your job is to identify the main ideas. The instructor may come back to the same few ideas again and again, so be alert to them.\n\nD  Listen for the signals. Good speakers use signals to show what they are going to say. Common signals are: to introduce an example, 'For example …' or 'There are three reasons why …'; to signal supporting material, 'For instance …', 'Similarly …', 'In contrast …' or 'On the other hand …'; to signal a conclusion or summary, 'Therefore …', 'In conclusion …', 'Finally …' or 'As a result …'; and to signal importance, 'Now this is very important …' or 'Remember that …'.\n\nE  Listening is not just soaking up sound. To be an effective listener, you must be active. It will help if you sit close enough to the instructor to see and hear easily. The further away you are, the greater the chance that the sound will be distorted, or that it will be disturbed by normal classroom noises, overhead projector fans, heaters or noises from outside the room.\n\nF  Another key to active listening is to keep eye contact. An instructor can tell whether you are 'getting it' or not simply by looking at you, and especially at your eyes. What is more, it is almost impossible to fall asleep while you are looking someone directly in the eye, so your ability to concentrate will improve! And respond to the instructor. This can be anything from asking and answering questions to nodding in understanding or smiling at the right moments when your instructor tries to be funny. Ask questions to keep your listening active.\n\nG  You should also make use of thought speed. Your mind works many times faster than the instructor can talk; some studies report that the brain works almost four times faster than normal speech, which often explains why daydreaming in lectures is so common. Think ahead to where the instructor is going with the lecture.\n\nH  Take notes. In ordinary conversation we mentally interpret, classify and summarise what is said. In the classroom we do this more effectively by keeping written notes. Note-taking helps us to listen by giving a logical organisation to what we hear. It is very difficult to listen to and remember disorganised, unrelated pieces of information. Organisation is the key to effective listening and remembering."
